  "id": "danza_de_diablos_costa_chica",
  "name": "Danza de los diablos (Costa Chica Afro-Mexican devil dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The danza de los diablos is the Afro-Mexican masked dance of the Costa Chica of Guerrero and Oaxaca, performed through the streets of towns such as Cuajinicuilapa and Collantes around the Days of the Dead by a troupe of devils with their leader and a man dressed as his wife. Its music comes from a harmonica, a donkey-jawbone rattle (charrasca) and the bote, a stick-friction drum, set against the dancers' stamping and shouts. The quijada card stands in for the charrasca and the friction-drum card for the bote.",
  "recipe": "classic modern danza de los diablos, ritual Devotional, Afro-diasporic branch. harmonica, jawbone rattle quijada, furruco. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
